-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/6578c7cb-8fea-5201-a4ce-d2a04d0e029e
-- title:
--   Finiteness of a Φ-fibre of coordinates inside an affinoid
-- statement:
--   The statement is set up over a complete discrete valuation datum and an associated Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient datum, and its conclusion is a finiteness assertion about one fibre of the uniformisation map.
--
--   **Arithmetic datum.** A prime $r$; a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal{O}$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi \in \mathcal{O}$ (`hπ`), with $\mathcal{O}$ complete for the $\pi$-adic topology (`hcomplete`), residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`) and $(r) = (\pi)$ in $\mathcal{O}$ (`hunr`, absolute unramifiedness); and a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is the fraction field of $\mathcal{O}$.
--
--   **Unramified coefficient ring.** A characteristic-zero domain $O^{nr}$ over $\mathcal{O}$ together with an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism $F_r$, subject to: $O^{nr}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{nr}$ satisfies some monic polynomial over $\mathcal{O}$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $O^{nr}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $F_r(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **Valuation of the determinant.** A group homomorphism $\mathrm{vdet} : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb{Z}$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $\mathrm{vdet}(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal{O}$.
--
--   **Group datum.** A group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma : G \to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and a subgroup $\Gamma \le G$ such that some $z \in \Gamma$ has $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix $c \cdot 1$ with $\mathrm{vdet}(\sigma z) = 2$ (`hcent`), and some $w \in \Gamma$ has $\mathrm{vdet}(\sigma w) = 1$ (`hodd`); a subgroup $\Gamma'$ characterised by $x \in \Gamma' \iff x \in \Gamma$ and $\mathrm{vdet}(\sigma x)$ is even (`hΓ'`). Further, a homomorphism $\rho : G \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ with $\rho g$ the class of $\sigma g$ (`hρ`), such that for every vertex $v$ of the lattice tree of $\mathcal{O}$ in $K_0$ the set of elements of $\rho(\Gamma')$ fixing $v$ is finite (`hdisc`, discreteness), and such that there is a finite set $S$ of vertices with every vertex carried into $S$ by some element of $\rho(\Gamma')$ (`hcocpt`, cocompactness).
--
--   **The scheme and the moduli interpretation.** A scheme $\mathcal{X}$ with a proper flat morphism $f : \mathcal{X} \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$, and a family $\Theta$ assigning, to each $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map from pairs consisting of an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $O^{nr} \to B$ and a Deligne datum of level $\pi$ over $B$, to the $B$-points of $\mathcal{X}$ over $\mathcal{O}$ (morphisms $\operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal{X}$ whose composite with $f$ is the structural morphism $\operatorname{Spec} B \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$). Here a Deligne datum over $B$ assigns to every full $\mathcal{O}$-lattice $M$ in $K_0^2$ a $B$-submodule of $B \otimes_{\mathcal{O}} M$ with invertible quotient, compatibly with inclusions of lattices and with homothety, and satisfying the nondegeneracy condition at every prime of $B$ recorded in `DeligneDatum.nondeg`. Four hypotheses constrain $\Theta$: naturality in $B$ along $\mathcal{O}$-algebra maps (`hΘnat`); invariance under the twisted action (`hΘinv`), namely $\Theta(x') = \Theta(x)$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma$ and `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` holds for $\sigma\gamma$, i.e. the $O^{nr}$-component of $x'$ is that of $x$ precomposed with $F_r^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\sigma\gamma)}$ and the Deligne datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$; the fibre description (`hΘfib`), which for every algebraically closed field $k$ over $\mathcal{O}$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent and every $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi : O^{nr} \to k$ asserts that every $k$-point of $\mathcal{X}$ over $\mathcal{O}$ is of the form $\Theta(\psi, P)$ and that $\Theta(\psi,P) = \Theta(\psi,P')$ holds precisely when $P$ and $P'$ are related by the twisted action of $\sigma\gamma$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma$; and the universal property (`hΘuniv`), which for every scheme $T$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}$ and every family $\rho'$ into the nilpotent-$\pi$ points of $T$ satisfying the same naturality and $\Gamma$-invariance produces a natural family $u$ of maps from the nilpotent-$\pi$ points of $f$ to those of $T$ with $u \circ \Theta = \rho'$, unique pointwise among natural families with this factorisation.
--
--   **Analytic frame.** An algebraically closed field $C$ over $K_0$, complete for a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$; a commutative ring $R$ over $\mathcal{O}$ with $C$ an $R$-algebra, the scalar towers $\mathcal{O} \to R \to C$ and $\mathcal{O} \to K_0 \to C$ being compatible; a pseudo-uniformiser $\varpi$, that is an element $\varpi.\varpi \in K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that for every nonzero $a \in K_0$ there is $N$ with $|\varpi|^{N} \le |a| \le |\varpi|^{-N}$; and `hF`, the assertion that $(\pi, \varpi, R)$ is an adic frame: $\pi$ is irreducible, $R \to C$ is injective with image exactly the elements of valuation $\le 1$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ of valuation $\le 1$ in $C$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal{O}$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and $\pi$ and $\varpi.\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. In addition: an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi_0 : O^{nr} \to R$; `hrk`, that for all $x, y \in C$ with $|x| < 1$ and $y \ne 0$ there is $n$ with $|x|^{n} \le |y|$; `hval`, that every nonzero $\varepsilon \in \Gamma_0$ dominates the valuation of some nonzero element of $C$; `hex`, that every point of the Drinfeld upper half plane $C \setminus K_0$ lies in the affinoid $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n$ for some $n$, where $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n$ consists of the $z \in C$ with $|z| \le |\varpi|^{-n}$ and $|z - a| \ge |\varpi|^{n}$ for every $a \in K_0$ with $|a| \le |\varpi|^{-n}$; and the assumption that the ring of functions holomorphic on all these affinoids, `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ`, is a domain.
--
--   **The uniformisation of adic points.** A map $\Phi$ from $\pi$-adic points over $R$ — compatible systems $(x.\mathrm{pt}\,n)_n$ of Deligne data over $R/(\pi^{n+1})$ — to $R$-points of $\mathcal{X}$ over $\mathcal{O}$, subject to the conjunction `hΦ` of four clauses: (a) for every adic point $x$ and every $n$, the reduction of $\Phi x$ along $R \to R/(\pi^{n+1})$ is the morphism underlying $\Theta$ evaluated at the pair consisting of $\psi_0$ followed by the quotient map and the level-$n$ component $x.\mathrm{pt}\,n$; (b) $\Phi$ is surjective; (c) $\Phi x = \Phi x'$ if and only if $x' = x.\mathrm{act}(\sigma\gamma)$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma'$; (d) base change along $R \to C$ is injective on $R$-points of $\mathcal{X}$ over $\mathcal{O}$, and every $C$-point of $\mathcal{X}$ over $\mathcal{O}$ arises by this base change from an $R$-point.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every adic point $x_0$ over $R$ and every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, the set of elements $z$ of the affinoid $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n$ for which there exists an adic point $x$ over $R$ with $z = x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ and $\Phi x = \Phi x_0$ is finite. Here $x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ is the coordinate of $x$: the unique $z \in C$ with $(z,1)$ lying in the $C$-line spanned by the standard line of $x$, when such a $z$ exists uniquely, and $0$ otherwise.
--
--   Inside the Čerednik–Drinfeld description of a proper flat curve over $\mathcal{O}$ as a quotient of the Drinfeld upper half plane, this is the local finiteness statement for the uniformisation: each fibre of $\Phi$, which by clause (c) is a $\Gamma'$-orbit, meets a given affinoid of the exhaustion in only finitely many coordinates. It is used in [`CerednikDrinfeld.finite_affinoid_toOmega_of_not_le_preimage_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient_of_smooth`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.finite_affinoid_toOmega_of_not_le_preimage_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient_of_smooth), where finiteness of such fibre intersections is needed to compare the analytic quotient with the scheme $\mathcal{X}$.
